WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.829 --> 00:01:28.990
Hundreds

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:42.340
of

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:48.060
people stood in a crowd in front of the Menlo
Park factory in New Jersey as a banner unfurled

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.700
to reveal the image of Thomas Edison.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.020
He wanted the world to know that he was the
most important person in the room.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.440
He was the brand.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:57.480
The inventor.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.760
The one in charge of over 1,000 machines created
under his name.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.040
Thomas Edison has become a polarizing figure
in modern history.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.500
Some believe that he was a genius inventor
and a brilliant businessman who changed the

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:11.980
world as we know it.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:18.030
Others believe that he was manipulative, and
stole the ideas of greater minds.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.030
The truth?

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.599
Well it's somewhere in between.

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:27.640
On today's Biographics, we are going to dive
into the incredible life of Thomas Edison.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:34.150
Early Life
Today, Thomas Edison is considered to be one

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.140
of the greatest American icons of all time.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:42.340
Surprisingly, though, his family history started
out as the exact opposite of the American

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.340
Dream.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.480
His grandfather, Samuel Ogden Edison, was
a loyalist during the Revolutionary War.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.310
So he decided to move the family to Canada,
and pledge his undying allegiance to King

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:53.310
George III.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:58.010
Thomas Edison’s father, Samuel Jr., was
later exiled from Canada for being apart of

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.230
the Mackenzie Rebellion.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:03.900
This was a revolt against the oligarchy who
was in control of local government.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.920
Sam Edison and his wife Nancy had no choice
but to move to the United States.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.190
They had a total of 7 children, but three
of them died during infancy.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:16.930
Thomas Alva Edison was the youngest child
in the family, and he was born in 1847 in

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.930
Milan, Ohio.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:23.050
He and his family moved to a farming community
in Port Huron, Michigan.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.300
He only had three months of formal schooling
before working on the family farm.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.480
His mother Nancy actually had several years
of education behind her from her time in Canada,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.070
so she taught him how to read and write at
home.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.480
Edison continued to read as many books as
he could get his hands on, and had a natural

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.040
curiosity for the world around him.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.780
He sat on goose eggs to see if he could get
them to hatch, and set a neighbor’s house

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.910
on fire, just to watch it burn...Because...you
know, we all know that with arson and property

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.650
damage, boys will be boys, right?

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.300
When reflecting on this time, Edison said
it was just a bunch of “devilish hijinks.”

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.390
Even as a young boy, Edison began to dabble
in chemical experiments in his parent’s

00:03:58.390 --> 00:03:59.390
basement.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:00.390
We’re pretty sure a basement isn’t the
best place to be mixing chemicals.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:03.020
He eventually got some fresh air by working
as a newspaper boy for a local railroad at

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.020
12 years old.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.069
Since he was spending so much time working,
he wanted to continue his experiments whenever

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:10.239
there was down time.

00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:14.840
So he began tinkering with his chemicals in
one of the train cars, and accidentally started

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:15.840
another fire.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.220
As you can tell, setting fire seems to be
a trend in his life.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.930
Not surprisingly, he was asked to pack up
his chemistry set and never return.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:30.020
Since becoming a newspaper boy didn’t work
out, Edison decided to learn a valuable skill.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.410
He taught himself morse code.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:35.360
No one ever taught him how to do this, so
Edison spent up to 18 hours a day sending

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.419
and receiving these messages in morse code
in order to become proficient at the skill.

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:43.259
By the time he turned 15, he got a full-time
job as a telegraph operator for one of the

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:44.330
local newspapers.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:48.569
He received the news from far-off publications,
and he was able to write down the basic information

00:04:48.569 --> 00:04:50.520
before handing it off to the writers.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.289
It was around this time when he began going
deaf.

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:57.439
It’s possible that the loud trains he worked
on as a young boy and the nearly constant

00:04:57.439 --> 00:05:02.330
tapping he had to listen to for most of his
life contributed to this very early hearing

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:03.330
loss.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.370
However, he was so hyper-focused on the task
at hand, as he tried to get better and better

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.240
at translating morse code.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.660
He was always highly competitive, and wanted
to be the best operator there.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.389
He once wrote that his hearing was so bad,
all of the background noise of other operators

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:18.419
faded away.

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:22.569
The sound of their machines was drowned out
in a low muffle, and the only thing he could

00:05:22.569 --> 00:05:25.360
hear was the tapping of his own telegraph.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.599
Because of this, he spent a lot of time thinking.

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:32.389
Instead of making friends at work, he scarcely
spoke to his co-workers, and concentrated

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:33.849
on the machines, instead.

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:37.189
Eventually, Thomas Edison asked to work the
night shift, so he was always doing overtime

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:38.830
a total of 16 hours a day.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.869
At night, there was scarcely any news coming
over the wire.

00:05:41.869 --> 00:05:45.199
So Edison had plenty of free time to tinker
with the machines.

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:48.039
He wanted to know how they worked, and how
they could possibly be improved.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:51.830
It wouldn’t be too long before he figured
out how to make his beloved telegraph machines

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.990
to the next level.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:06:01.719
The Great American Inventor
When he was 22 years old, Thomas Edison decided

00:06:01.719 --> 00:06:05.050
to leave his job as a telegraph operator and
moved to New York City.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:09.969
At this point, he had studied the telegraph
machines so closely that he knew how to manufacture

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:14.349
them from scratch, and he made far more money
creating and selling the machines that he

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:15.940
did as a mere operator.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.979
New York City right in the midst of the industrial
revolution.

00:06:18.979 --> 00:06:23.930
Movie stars did not exist yet, so the real
heroes of America were the inventors.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:28.210
Everyone wished that they could come up with
a brilliant new idea and file a patent, which

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.190
would lead to fame and fortune.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:34.970
Their name would be published in the newspapers
and magazines, and they became a permanent

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:36.159
part of history.

00:06:36.159 --> 00:06:40.319
For years, Americans had struggled to find
their sense of national identity.

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:44.029
They didn't have the great architecture or
universities that you could find in Europe,

00:06:44.029 --> 00:06:48.819
and many people considered most things in
the young nation to be sub-par.

00:06:48.819 --> 00:06:53.520
But once Americans became the masters of innovation,
many young men from all over the world began

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.999
to immigrate to New York City in order to
become the next great inventor.

00:06:57.999 --> 00:07:02.339
Edison began to receive more and more orders
for new telegraph machines, and he had a good

00:07:02.339 --> 00:07:03.849
business going for himself.

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:08.159
He started to hire other men to put the pieces
together, in order to have more time to tinker

00:07:08.159 --> 00:07:09.159
on his own.

00:07:09.159 --> 00:07:12.360
As time went on, he began to create telegraphs
that could print out messages.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.500
He also developed the patent for a multiplex
telegraph, which could send out several messages

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:18.500
at the same time.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.749
After making these new machines, he only attracted
more and more customers to order from him

00:07:22.749 --> 00:07:24.660
over the competition.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.740
In 1871, he was 24 years old when he met his
wife, Mary Stilwell, and got married.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.069
He was 24 years old, and she was just 16.

00:07:32.069 --> 00:07:36.869
They had two children together, and she immediately
set out helping him with his meals, clothing,

00:07:36.869 --> 00:07:41.210
and other essentials so that he could continue
to concentrate on work.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.770
Edison worked throughout the day and night
to fulfill his orders.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:47.740
He scarcely stopping to sleep or eat, because
there was so much to do.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:52.069
He once said, “The reason a lot of people
do not recognize opportunity is because it

00:07:52.069 --> 00:07:56.629
usually goes around wearing overalls looking
like hard work.”

00:07:56.629 --> 00:08:01.210
After a few years of this hardcore lifestyle,
Thomas Edison began to make a lot of money

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:02.580
from his telegraph business.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.209
At 29 years old, he invested nearly all of
his earnings from those 7 years into a laboratory

00:08:07.209 --> 00:08:08.560
in Menlo Park, New Jersey.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.090
He hired a team of men who all shared a vision
that Menlo Park could become the world’s

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:14.090
first “think tank”.

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:17.129
It was a place where inventions could happen.

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:20.929
He wanted to be the world’s very first full-time
professional inventor.

00:08:20.929 --> 00:08:25.569
His family moved into a house that was built
on the same property as the factory.

00:08:25.569 --> 00:08:29.999
Even though he was working close to his wife
and children, they almost never saw him, because

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:32.089
he spent almost all of his time working.

00:08:32.089 --> 00:08:34.690
At night, he would walk home to sleep.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:39.310
The one and only day he took off was Sunday,
for church and rest.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.740
Edison once wrote to a friend that he had
to goal of creating a minor invention every

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.089
10 days, and a major breakthrough once a month.

00:08:45.089 --> 00:08:48.160
Of course, this would be impossible for one
man to do alone.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.340
He would give his employees assignments to
work on specific projects, or ask them to

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:54.100
find a solution to a problem.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.680
Since he had multiple teams working on these
new invention every single day, they were

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.970
able to come up with new breakthroughs.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:04.120
Once they finished a new invention, he would
patent the idea in his own name, and reap

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.680
the benefits of its profits.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.830
in 1876, there was a massive exhibition in
Philadelphia where inventors showcased their

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:11.830
new ideas.

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:16.030
This is where Alexander Graham Bell displayed
the very first telephone.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:18.440
This invention threatened Thomas Edison's
livelihood.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.620
It would make the telegraph completely obsolete.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:25.070
After all, if you could hear someone's voice,
why would you bother learning morse code to

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.860
communicate over the telegraph?

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.870
Thomas Edison immediately decided to try to
improve on the telephone’s design.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.630
He once said, "An inventor needs an enemy."

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.970
Bell was from a well-to-do family and he had
a college education.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:40.830
He also had plenty of money handed to him
from his father-in-law to tinker with his

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.040
new inventions.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:46.430
Edison was motivated to compete with him,
and just a few months later, his laboratory

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:51.160
developed an improved version of Bell's telephone
that could be patented.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:55.020
He had once figured out how to write from
a telegraph, based on the vibrations coming

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:56.200
from the Morse code.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.450
He thought- if it worked on paper, why not
something else?

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:04.110
He placed a needle on top of a sheet of foil,
and began to speak loudly into the phonograph.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:09.530
The first thing he ever recorded was the child's
nursery rhyme, "Mary Had a Little Lamb".

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:14.150
After years of improving on other people’s
inventions, his kaleidoscopic mind had finally

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.080
rearranged all of the knowledge he had collected.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.380
This was his first completely original invention.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.050
"I was taken aback," he said.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.410
"I was always afraid of anything that worked
the first time."

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.800
1877, Scientific American published the story
about Edison's phonograph.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:34.180
Reporters from all over the world rushed to
Menlo Park to see the new device first hand.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.070
Edison knew how to be charismatic, and played
to the crowd.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:41.070
When the reporters would show, up he would
offer them slices of pie and cigars while

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.040
he told them entertaining stories.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.220
This made the reporters like him, and they
would write about his brilliant mind and impressive

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.220
laboratory.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:51.550
Like any businessman who knows what he’s
doing you can bet that he immediately took

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.840
full advantage of all of this free press.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.440
He ran ads in newspapers offering to sell
anyone a phonograph to anyone in America.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:01.940
Thousands of people would have read the news
and want to order these machines for their

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:02.940
own homes.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:07.800
Now, instead of just producing telegraphs,
the Edison factory was also selling telephones

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:11.250
and phonographs, and he became incredibly
wealthy.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.391
Newspapers continue to print stories about
his personal life, because they believed that

00:11:14.391 --> 00:11:18.770
he was exactly what they would imagine as
the perfect All-American inventor.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:22.340
On April Fool's Day, one newspaper printed
a story that Thomas Edison had come up with

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:26.210
an invention to turn dirt into food and wine.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:30.480
Even though this was completely ridiculous,
people actually fell for it because they thought

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.030
that he truly could invent anything.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:36.960
He began receiving fan letters and interview
requests on a daily basis.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.400
People would ask for money, favors, and just
about anything you could think of.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:46.170
Edison spent a significant amount of everyday
answering these letters and speaking to reporters,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.040
which took even more of his free time away
from his family.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:53.200
After a while, being a public figure became
so overwhelming that he was no longer getting

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.870
any work done in the factory at all.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:12:00.410
In 1878, Thomas Edison decided to go on a
trip to Wyoming so that he and a group of

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:02.570
men could watch the solar eclipse.

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:05.640
His wife Mary was pregnant with their third
child.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:10.640
She felt sick, and became very anxious, especially
with so many people knocking on their door

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:11.990
looking for her husband.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:16.000
Doctors wrote to Edison that he needed to
return home immediately, because she was too

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.920
ill to be left alone.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.540
Instead of rushing back to his pregnant wife,
Edison ignored the letters.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:25.330
He was used to Mary complaining that he worked
too much, and he usually ignored her please

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:26.330
for help.

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:28.610
So he finished out 3 more weeks of his trip.

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.030
Sadly, Mary was more than just anxious.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:32.030
She truly was sick.

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:41.391
For the next few years, her health only continued
to decline.

00:12:41.391 --> 00:12:43.000
The War of Currents
Soon after the birth of his third child, Thomas

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:46.040
Edison did the opposite of slowing down and
enjoying his time as a father.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:49.770
He began his most ambitious project yet; The
light bulb.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:53.500
Plenty of other inventors had come up with
light bulbs before, but they burned out after

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:54.700
just a few minutes.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:56.680
So he decided to come up with a solution.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:01.030
In just a few days Thomas Edison figured out
how to have a long-lasting incandescent light

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.030
bulb.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.870
Since the journalists were waiting with bated
breath to find out what he would do next,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.470
this became an overnight sensation.

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:13.080
Investors were suddenly showing up to his
door handing in money, and Edison stock went

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:14.240
through the roof.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.510
They wanted to support his idea to create
a society that no longer needed to rely on

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.480
dirty oil lamps to bring light to people's
homes.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:27.050
He announced that he would do the impossible:
Light up New York City so that it shines even

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:28.050
at night.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:32.410
But by the summer of 1879, New York City still
was not filled with electric light bulbs as

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:33.640
promised.

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:38.270
Edison stock was dropping, and people accused
him of lying to manipulate the market.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:42.910
People began to lose hope that an electric
city could ever exist.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:47.100
Behind the scenes, workers in Menlo Park were
hustling day and night to get the light bulbs

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.100
working.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:51.150
Of course, the task was much more difficult
than Edison had ever anticipated.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:55.410
They needed sockets, wiring, and an electrical
system that must be installed into a building

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:57.220
before appliances could work.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.480
They also needed to create massive generators.

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:03.130
He wanted to achieve this using DC, or Direct
Current.

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:07.520
However, the DC power generations broke down
very easily, and he needed to hire someone

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.400
to come up with a solution to the problem.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:13.200
His saving grace was none other than Nikola
Tesla.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.390
He was the true natural-born genius with an
“eidetic memory”, which meant that he

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:21.080
could recall objects from memory, and recreate
them perfectly.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.930
After spending years working on DC power in
Budapest, 28-year old Nikola Tesla decided

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:28.310
to leave Europe on a boat to New York City.

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:33.680
After hearing so much hype up about the American
Dream, Tesla was actually disappointed once

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:34.680
he arrived.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.050
He said, "What I had left [in Europe] was
beautiful, artistic and fascinating in every

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:42.880
way; what I saw here was machined, rough and
unattractive.

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:48.010
It [America] is a century behind Europe in
civilization."

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:53.220
Despite being a bit underwhelmed by the scenery,
he was still caught up in the enthusiasm for

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:54.240
invention.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.710
Almost immediately after arriving to the United
States, he approached Thomas Edison and asked

00:14:58.710 --> 00:14:59.710
for a job.

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:04.160
Once Tesla learned about the issues with the
DC generator, Edison made him a deal He said

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:09.190
that if he could come up with a solution to
the problem, he promised to give him a $50,000

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:10.230
bonus.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:14.740
With modern inflation, that’s closer to
1 and a half million dollars.

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:18.060
Edison truly underestimated what a hungry
genius can accomplish with that kind of money

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:19.060
on the line.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:21.830
In only a few months, Tesla fixed all of the
DC generators.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:26.330
When he came to collect his $50,000, reward,
Edison brushed it off, saying that it was

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:27.330
a joke.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:31.800
He blamed it on Tesla’s inability to understand
the “American sense of humor”.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.070
He wasn’t falling for it, though.

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:36.860
Nikola Tesla immediately quit his job, and
decided to go to business on his own.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:41.310
When he was repairing Edison’s generators,
he could see all of the problems, so invented

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:45.480
a much better version called “Alternating
Current”, or AC Power.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:49.520
Edison’s Direct Current could only move
in one direction, and there needed to be generators

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.630
every mile in order for the system to work.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:56.180
But Tesla’s Alternating Current bounced
back and forth, and it could vary in voltage.

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:59.040
This meant it could go hundreds of miles in-between
generators.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:02.480
Tesla patented the technology, but it would
be an expensive undertaking.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:07.430
He tried to show it to investors, but most
thought it was impossible, and weren’t willing

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:08.790
to compete with Edison.

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:11.880
That was, until Tesla met George Westinghouse.

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:16.660
Together, they spent the next few years plotting
to create a system that would defeat Thomas

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:18.320
Edison’s electric lights.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:21.920
In the midst of this Current War, Edison’s
wife, Mary, died in 1888.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:23.300
She was just 29 years old.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:27.100
The doctors say that she had “congestion
of the brain”, which was a blanket term

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:31.680
given to such an intense level of depression
and anxiety that it lead to high blood pressure

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:32.680
and a stroke.

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:37.190
When she first met him, Mary was just a 16-year
old girl who married a man with a small telegraph

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:38.190
business.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:42.060
There was nothing that could have prepared
her for the pressure of being the wife of

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:43.060
a celebrity.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:46.480
Since Edison was devoted to his company nearly
24/7, it only makes sense that he met his

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.100
second wife at work.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:51.530
Two years after Mary’s death, Thomas Edison
married a woman named Mina Miller.

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:55.970
Her father was also a well-known inventor,
and she had already grown up around famous

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:56.970
scientists.

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:00.260
This inspired her to work in one of Edison’s
factories in Newark.

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:05.829
She had a keen scientific mind, and completely
understood Thomas Edison’s passion for innovation.

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:09.480
During interviews, he would openly talk about
how much he loved and appreciated her.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:13.699
Edison even bought her a luxurious mansion
called Glenmont in the nearby town of West

00:17:13.699 --> 00:17:14.699
Orange.

00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:17.140
Mina was exactly the kind of wife Edison needed.

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:21.299
She was able to manage their social calendar
to have parties with important people in the

00:17:21.299 --> 00:17:25.189
industry, and this only improved his relationships
with investors.

00:17:25.189 --> 00:17:30.110
However, while Edison and Mina were schmoozing
with investors, Tesla and Westinghouse were

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:33.149
still hard at work developing their Alternating
Current.

00:17:33.149 --> 00:17:39.330
In 1893, Tesla and Westinghouse debuted the
AC Power system at the Chicago World’s Fair.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:43.910
Over 100,000 light bulbs illuminated the night,
and people were in total awe.

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:46.200
It was clear who the better inventor truly
was.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:50.799
Tesla and Westinghouse won the contract to
light up New York City.

00:17:50.799 --> 00:17:55.630
After this, Edison was furious, and began
one of the worst smear campaigns in history.

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:58.919
He claimed that AC power was too dangerous
for people’s homes.

00:17:58.919 --> 00:18:03.460
He demonstrated this by electrocuting animals
in public- even elephants.

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:07.509
Edison was also the man behind demonstrating
how human beings could be killed in the electric

00:18:07.509 --> 00:18:08.509
chair.

00:18:08.509 --> 00:18:13.129
However, all of his attempts to ruin Tesla
and Westinghouse didn’t work, because AC

00:18:13.129 --> 00:18:17.429
power prevailed, and the War of Currents was
over.

00:18:17.429 --> 00:18:25.690
Motion Pictures and a Lasting Legacy
Thomas Edison could not make his mark on the

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:28.440
world as being the man who would light up
the world.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:33.269
However, he succeeded in introducing the motion
picture camera to the United States.

00:18:33.269 --> 00:18:38.139
In 1888, Edison He told the press, "I am experimenting
upon an instrument which does for the eye

00:18:38.139 --> 00:18:43.769
what the phonograph does for the ear, which
is the recording and reproduction of things

00:18:43.769 --> 00:18:44.769
in motion ...."

00:18:44.769 --> 00:18:49.830
A Frenchman named Louis Le Prince had developed
the first motion picture camera in France.

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:54.169
So Edison tasked one of his employees, William
Kennedy Laurie Dickson, to improve it just

00:18:54.169 --> 00:18:55.770
enough to file a patent.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:56.770
The result was the Kinetograph camera.

00:18:56.770 --> 00:19:01.120
They also created the Kinetoscope, which was
a device where people had to look into a view-finder

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.749
to see the moving pictures after they had
been captured.

00:19:04.749 --> 00:19:09.090
Edison began to sell his new devices to the
masses, just like he had with his phonographs.

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:13.230
Edison’s lab also began to double as a filming
studio, because they wanted to sell these

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.539
reels for people to watch on their Kinetoscopes.

00:19:15.539 --> 00:19:19.809
They began to release productions of short
films, and charged people for tickets to see

00:19:19.809 --> 00:19:21.049
these new “movies”.

00:19:21.049 --> 00:19:25.299
However, once other filmmakers in New York
City started to produce films, Edison would

00:19:25.299 --> 00:19:26.480
try to sue them.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:30.460
He wanted a monopoly over any movie that was
made with his devices.

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:35.679
So, these film makers decided it was time
to get as far away as humanly possible.

00:19:35.679 --> 00:19:36.679
They went to Hollywood.

00:19:36.679 --> 00:19:40.950
So, if it wasn’t for Thomas Edison’s litigious
nature, the movie capital of the world would

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:43.740
probably still be in New York City.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:49.350
Over the course of his lifetime, Thomas Edison
filed 1,093 patents for new inventions.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:54.139
He died in 1931 at his home in West Orange,
New Jersey at the age of 84.

00:19:54.139 --> 00:19:59.389
Today, many people have discredited Edison,
because they believe that he doesn’t actually

00:19:59.389 --> 00:20:02.750
deserve all of the praise he was given over
the years.

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:06.700
After hearing all of the terrible stories
about him, it’s easy to see why.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:11.450
However, you cannot deny that this man truly
did leave a lasting legacy, and he inspired

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:14.200
countless numbers of inventors and businessmen.

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:18.820
You could also argue that nearly every inventor
does similar things that Edison had.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:23.330
No one is angry at Elon Musk for the fact
that he was not the first to invent a car

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:24.330
or a rocket ship.

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:28.700
They’re also not mad that he has a huge
staff in his factory working for him, or that

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:29.919
he has a public image.

00:20:29.919 --> 00:20:34.149
So, do you think Thomas Edison was unfairly
criticized for his competitive nature?

00:20:34.149 --> 00:20:38.700
Or was he truly just a terrible person who
deserves all the hate?

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:53.999
Let us know in

00:20:53.999 --> 00:21:07.149
the comments.

